# G030 — Text to HTML Generator

*v2. See `text-to-html.md` for prose: format translation as identity
transformation (content preserved, representation changed), markdown-
to-HTML is the vault's rendering pipeline, inline formatting as
pattern-based `@map` over matches.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Rendering Pipeline

[vault-render @note-path
    IN ORDER TO
        read a markdown note, translate to HTML
        and hand the rendered output to whichever consumer requested it
        so the same content serves authoring (markdown) and display (HTML)

    @text <- [fs[read-string {path: @note-path}]]
    @html <- [text-to-html {text: @text}]

    concurrent [
        [ui[quickshell-display {html: @html}]]
    |
        [ui[web-publish {path: @note-path, html: @html}]]
    |
        [cache[store {path: @note-path, html: @html}]]
    ]
    join
]

---

## Verb Surface

[text-to-html
    {text}                                   ;; markdown -> HTML
]

[apply-inline
    {text}                                    ;; inline transformations only
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: format translation `where` content is invariant

[text-to-html {text: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        produce HTML that says exactly what @t says in markdown
        so the difference is audience (authors vs renderers), not meaning

    parse @t into an intermediate document model
        where paragraph-break is blank line
        where heading is line starting with #+ space
        where list item is line starting with * or -
        where code block is fence ``` ... ```
    emit HTML from the model
        where heading level is count of leading #
        where lists wrap in <ul>/<ol>
        where inline formatting applies per match
]

Same normalise-to-canonical pattern G004 (base) and G010 (unit)
use at value-level, G030 uses at document-level. The intermediate
model is the canonical form; markdown and HTML are both projections.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: inline formatting as pattern `where` match applies

[apply-inline {text: @t}]
    scan @t for each pattern
        where **text**       -> <strong>text</strong>
        where *text*          -> <em>text</em>
        where `text`          -> <code>text</code>
        where [text](url)     -> <a href="url">text</a>
    IN ORDER TO
        treat inline formatting as G032's regex-over-patterns
        where each pattern is a transformation rule with positional context
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [text-to-html
          {text: "# Heading\n\n**bold** and *italic* words"}]
    ;; -> "<h1>Heading</h1><p><strong>bold</strong> and <em>italic</em> words</p>"

    -> [apply-inline {text: "a **bold** word"}]
    ;; -> "a <strong>bold</strong> word"
]
